content.text:
Now big concern: The user may expect "more maps" and enemy rosters differ. Achieved. Need ensure audio isn't broken by new enemies; audio methods generic.

Let's test all maps visually and end-to-end via a script that starts each wave and runs some simulation frames, checking no console errors. To run each wave quickly we can call `g.update(0.1)` many times in eval, but render isn't called. Errors in render will be caught by game loop if we let it run. Better use actual wave UI and game loop at speed 3 for each map, but that takes time. Could call `g.render()` in a loop after forcing enemies. Do a loop over all enemy types and render each into canvas. Use `g.spawnEnemy` with specs from startWave queue for wave 25 and then call render. We can spawn all specs sequentially and render each? Actually drawEnemy called for all enemies in render. Spawn all final wave specs (via g.spawnEnemy) and then render. Any error would throw in eval and we'd see. Let's test. Note `g.spawnEnemy` increments waveSpawned and pushes; for each map, wave 25 specs. Use g.startWave to get specs, then spawn all (or first 20) and call g.render. This exercises all enemy bodies, including bosses. Also spawn all at t=0 overlapping but rendering fine.

Test:
```
for id:
 g.setMap(id); g.begin(); g.waveNumber=25; g.phase='build'; g.waveActive=false; g.spawnQueue=null; g.enemies=[]; g.bossRef=null; g.startWave();
 const specs = g.spawnQueue.slice(); g.spawnQueue=[]; // avoid update?
 for s of specs g.spawnEnemy(s);
 g.render();
 out counts: enemies.length, types
```
Note spawnEnemy expects spec with delay etc irrelevant. Boss effects and audio maybe audio context not initialized but methods likely handle. In headless no gesture, audio engine may suspend gracefully. Let's run.

Works.

Now test a full wave clear via fast-forward. We can set game.speed 3 and wait maybe wave takes ~20s. Could call `g.update(30)` but update caps dt? `update(rawDt)` uses `Math.min(rawDt, 0.15)` so can't fast-forward 30 sec in one call. Loop `for (i=0;i<600;i++) g.update(0.05)` in eval; but game loop also runs. That's 30 seconds sim; in eval synchronous. Set g.speed=1. We can run 600 steps; spawn queue 9 units etc. Tower may not kill all, but eventually leak all; maybe lives drops. Need test completeWave. Could just kill all enemies after spawning: run steps until queue empty, then kill all with damageEnemy trueDamage, then update. But cleanup + complete condition in stepSim. Let's do a scripted test:
```
g.setMap('hoth'); g.begin(); g.credits=1e6; place some towers; g.startWave(); 
for (let i=0;i<400 && (g.spawnQueue?.length || g.enemies.length);i++){ g.update(0.05); if (g.enemies.length) for (const e of g.enemies) g.damageEnemy(e, 1e9, {trueDamage:true});}
return {phase,wave,credits,...}
```
But killing enemies requires weird. Simpler use no towers, and loop `g.update(0.05)` 1200 times until all enemies leak, then lives -> 0 defeat, not wave clear. To test complete, make invulnerable base by setting lives 999, then let all leak, check wave complete. `g.lives=999`; loop update until waveActive false. This tests wave completion and wave increment. Let's do.

